\begin_layout Abstract
The growing complexity of modern genomic data analysis requires interoperability between different computational environments.
 While Bioconductor has established itself as a the framework for genomic data analysis in R,
 the Python ecosystem has gained significant traction in data science,
 machine learning,
 and artificial intelligence.
 Here,
 we present BiocPy,
 a comprehensive suite of Python packages that ports core Bioconductor data structures that maintain compatibility with their R counterparts.
 BiocPy facilitates seamless cross-language workflows,
 enabling researchers to leverage the strengths of both ecosystems.
 It implements foundational data structures such as BiocFrame,
 GenomicRanges,
 SummarizedExperiment,
 and SingleCellExperiment,
 alongside utilities for direct reading of R data objects via the rds2py package.
 BiocPy enables researchers to combine the statistical rigor of Bioconductor with Python's machine learning capabilities,
 offering a powerful framework for genomic analyses.
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to Python's Package Index (PyPI) 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "http://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bioinformatics workflows increasingly require the integration of diverse computational tools and methods.
 Bioconductor
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "huber2015orchestrating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 with its extensive collection of R packages for genomic data analysis,
 has been the widely used by researchers in the field for nearly two decades.
 It provides a cohesive framework with standardized data representations and analysis tools that allow researchers to seamlessly manipulate and analyze genomic data across multiple workflows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Meanwhile,
 Python has emerged as the dominant language for data science,
 machine learning,
 and artificial intelligence applications.
 Its extensive ecosystem of standard libraries like NumPy,
 pandas,
 pyTorch and TensorFlow along with a strong community around imaging,
 single-cell
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Virshup2021.12.16.473007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and machine learning methods has made it indispensable for modern computational biology.
 However,
 Python lacks standardized genomic data structures equivalent to those in Bioconductor,
 leading to fragmentation and interoperability challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This presents a significant challenge:
 researchers often need to choose between Bioconductor's robust statistical methods and genomic tools in R,
 or Python's superior machine learning capabilities and broader scientific computing ecosystem.
 While bridging solutions such as rpy2
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rpy2"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 exist,
 they require proficiency in both languages and introduce complexity in deployment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address this gap,
 we developed BiocPy,
 a collection of packages that bring Bioconductor's core data structures to Python.
 BiocPy enables seamless interoperability between R and Python,
 allowing researchers to leverage the strengths of both ecosystems within a unified analytical framework.
 The project includes implementations of fundamental Bioconductor data structures such as 
\emph on
BiocFrame
\emph default
 for R-like dataframes,
 
\emph on
GenomicRanges
\emph default
 for range-based analyses,
 containers for managing experimental data,
 e.g.
 
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
,
 and 
\emph on
SingleCellExperiment
\emph default
,
 along with utilities for direct reading of R data objects through the 
\emph on
rds2py
\emph default
 package.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike other Python bioinformatics libraries that create their own data models,
 BiocPy prioritizes interoperability with Bioconductor by adhering to its design principles.
 This approach enables seamless transition between languages and unlocks new possibilities for integrated and cross language genomic analyses.
 Creating a bridge between computational platforms enables researches to focus their time on biological questions rather than technical challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Core Data Structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy implements essential foundational data structures that serve as building blocks for complex genomic data representations.
 These structures closely mirror their Bioconductor counterparts while integrating naturally with the Python ecosystem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
BiocFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocFrame provides a Bioconductor-like data frame class in Python that follows the principle of "what you put is what you get." Unlike pandas DataFrame,
 which modifies column types during input,
 BiocFrame preserves the original data types.
 This is particularly important when representing complex genomic data structures and ensuring interoperability with R/Bioconductor.
 Key differences from pandas include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Preservation of column data types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Support for nested data structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Compatibility with R/Bioconductor's 
\emph on
DataFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocFrame also provides seamless conversion to and from pandas DataFrame objects,
 allowing researchers to leverage pandas' rich functionality for data manipulation while maintaining compatibility with Bioconductor workflows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GenomicRanges package implements interval-based genomic coordinate representations,
 essential for many genomic analyses.
 It includes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
IRanges for generic interval arithmetic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GenomicRanges for representing genomic locations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SeqInfo for storing chromosome information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Methods for interval operations (overlap,
 nearest,
 etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This implementation enables Python users to perform the same range-based genomic analyses that have been a cornerstone of Bioconductor workflows in R.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Container Classes for Experimental Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy includes container classes for representing experimental data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SummarizedExperiment:
 Represents genomic experiments with features (rows) and samples (columns),
 integrating assay data with annotation metadata
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
RangedSummarizedExperiment:
 Extends SummarizedExperiment with genomic coordinate information for each feature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SingleCellExperiment:
 Represents single-cell experimental data,
 with additional slots for reduced dimensions,
 alternative experiments,
 and cell-cell/feature-feature relationships
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
MultiAssayExperiment:
 Manages multi-omic experiments across multiple samples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These container classes follow Bioconductor's design principles where rows represent features and columns represent samples or cells,
 ensuring conceptual consistency across languages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Interoperability Features
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Direct Reading of RDS Files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
rds2py
\emph default
 package serves as a Python interface to the 
\emph on
rds2cpp
\emph default
 library,
 enabling direct reading of RDS files in Python without intermediate formats.
 This eliminates data conversion steps and streamlines the transition between R and Python environments.
 Key features include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Support for Bioconductor S4 classes (SummarizedExperiment,
 SingleCellExperiment,
 etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Memory-efficient implementation through memory views,
 avoiding data duplication
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Direct conversion to BiocPy objects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Conversion Utilities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy provides bidirectional conversion utilities for compatibility with other Python ecosystem tools:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conversion between BiocFrame and pandas DataFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Import/export capabilities for AnnData objects (used by Scanpy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Support for common file formats (CSV,
 HDF5,
 etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Installation and Deployment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All BiocPy packages are published to PyPI and can be installed individually or through the biocpy wrapper package.
 Conda/Mamba configuration files are also provided for creating environments with all BiocPy dependencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Package Design Principles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy follows several key design principles:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Faithful Representation:
 BiocPy classes implement the same interfaces and behaviors as their Bioconductor counterparts,
 ensuring conceptual consistency across languages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Functional Programming Paradigm:
 Like Bioconductor,
 BiocPy uses functional programming patterns for accessing and setting properties,
 avoiding in-place modifications that can lead to unexpected behavior in complex modular data structures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Python-Native Implementation:
 While maintaining compatibility with Bioconductor,
 BiocPy is implemented as a native Python library,
 following Python best practices and integrating with the broader Python ecosystem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Minimal Dependencies:
 BiocPy maintains a lean dependency footprint,
 using standard scientific Python libraries (NumPy,
 SciPy,
 pandas) while avoiding unnecessary dependencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Comprehensive Documentation:
 Each package includes detailed documentation,
 tutorials,
 and examples to facilitate adoption by the Python bioinformatics community.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Results (#Usecases)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
BiocPy facilitates seamless cross-language workflows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the primary advantages of BiocPy is its ability to enable seamless workflows that span both the R and Python ecosystems.
 This interoperability allows researchers to leverage the statistical rigor of Bioconductor alongside Python's machine learning capabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following sections demonstrate how BiocPy can be used in practical research scenarios to bridge the gap between R/Bioconductor and Python-based analysis pipelines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Reading Bioconductor objects directly in Python
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rds2py package facilitates direct reading of Bioconductor objects stored in RDS files,
 eliminating the need for intermediate file formats or data conversion steps.
 This capability is demonstrated in the following example,
 where we read a SummarizedExperiment object from an RDS file:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,numbers=left"
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from rds2py import read_rds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from summarizedexperiment import as_summarized_experiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Read the RDS file containing a SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

r_obj = read_rds("path/to/summarized_experiment.rds")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Convert to a BiocPy SummarizedExperiment object
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

se = as_summarized_experiment(r_obj)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Access the data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Dimensions:
 {se.shape}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Assay names:
 {se.get_assay_names()}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Row metadata columns:
 {se.get_row_data().get_column_names()}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Column metadata columns:
 {se.get_column_data().get_column_names()}")
\end_layout

\end_inset

This approach is particularly valuable for organizations with extensive Bioconductor-based data repositories,
 as it allows them to access and analyze these data in Python without migration or conversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Integration with ArtifactDB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy integrates with ArtifactDB,
 a language-agnostic system for storing and retrieving computational artifacts.
 This allows researchers to access data objects across different programming environments:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from biocpy.artifactdb import fetch_artifact
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from singlecellexperiment import SingleCellExperiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Fetch a SingleCellExperiment stored in ArtifactDB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce = fetch_artifact("my-project/single-cell-experiment:v1")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Access and analyze the data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Number of cells:
 {sce.shape[1]}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Number of genes:
 {sce.shape[0]}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(f"Available reduced dimensions:
 {sce.get_reduced_dim_names()}")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Case study:
 scRNA-seq analysis with scRNAseq and celldex packages
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following example demonstrates a complete cross-language workflow for single-cell RNA sequencing analysis,
 leveraging both Bioconductor packages and Python's machine learning capabilities:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# In R (for reference):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# library(scRNAseq)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# library(SingleCellExperiment)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# sce <- ZeiselBrainData()
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# saveRDS(sce,
 "zeisel_brain.rds")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# In Python:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from rds2py import read_rds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from singlecellexperiment import as_single_cell_experiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

import numpy as np
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from sklearn.decomposition import PCA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from umap import UMAP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Read the SCE object created in R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

r_obj = read_rds("zeisel_brain.rds")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce = as_single_cell_experiment(r_obj)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Access normalized expression data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

normalized = sce.get_assay("logcounts")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Perform dimensionality reduction with scikit-learn
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

pca = PCA(n_components=20)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

pca_results = pca.fit_transform(normalized.T)  # Transpose for cell-wise PCA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Add the PCA results to the SCE object
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce.set_reduced_dim("PCA",
 pca_results)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Perform UMAP for visualization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

umap = UMAP(n_components=2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

umap_results = umap.fit_transform(pca_results)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce.set_reduced_dim("UMAP",
 umap_results)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Cluster the cells using KMeans
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=10,
 random_state=0)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

clusters = kmeans.fit_predict(pca_results)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Add cluster assignments to the column data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

col_data = sce.get_column_data().add_column("kmeans_cluster",
 clusters)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce = sce.set_column_data(col_data)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Save the results for further analysis in either R or Python
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from singlecellexperiment import write_h5ad
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

write_h5ad(sce,
 "zeisel_brain_analyzed.h5ad")
\end_layout

\end_inset

This example demonstrates how BiocPy enables researchers to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Read data processed in R/Bioconductor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Perform analysis using Python's machine learning libraries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Store results in a format accessible by both ecosystems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
BiocPy implements bindings to methods available in R/Bioconductor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy provides Python bindings to high-performance analysis methods implemented in C++ libraries that are also used by Bioconductor packages.
 This approach allows Python users to access the same computational methods without reimplementation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Integration with libscran for single-cell analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The scranpy package provides Python bindings to libscran,
 a C++ library that implements efficient methods for single-cell RNA-seq analysis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

import numpy as np
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from scipy import sparse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from scranpy.normalization import LogNormCounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from singlecellexperiment import SingleCellExperiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from biocframe import BiocFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Create a sparse count matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

nrows = 5000  # genes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ncols = 1000  # cells
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

counts = sparse.random(nrows,
 ncols,
 density=0.1,
 format="csr",
 dtype=np.float32)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Create a SingleCellExperiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce = SingleCellExperiment(assays={"counts":
 counts})
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Perform log-normalization using libscran
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

norm = LogNormCounts()
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

normalized = norm(sce.get_assay("counts"))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# Add the normalized data to the SCE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

sce.set_assay("logcounts",
 normalized)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

